::ALG1_U02_L01_GP01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L01_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x + 6 = 14. {
= x = 8#Correct. Subtract 6 from both sides: x = 8.
~ x = 20#This adds 6 instead of undoing addition by subtracting 6.
~ x = -8#This has the right distance but the wrong sign. Check by substitution.
~ x = 6#This repeats the added number instead of solving for the variable.
}

::ALG1_U02_L01_GP02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L01_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Solve m - 9 = 4. {
~ m = -5#This subtracts 9 again. Add 9 to both sides to undo subtraction.
= m = 13#Correct. Add 9 to both sides: m = 13.
~ m = 5#This uses the difference between 9 and 4, not the value that makes the equation true.
~ m = 36#This uses multiplication, but the equation requires addition.
}

::ALG1_U02_L01_GP03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L01_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Solve -3p = 18. {
~ p = 15#This subtracts 3, but -3 is multiplying the variable.
~ p = 6#This divides by 3 but loses the negative sign.
= p = -6#Correct. Divide both sides by -3: p = -6.
~ p = -54#This multiplies instead of dividing.
}

::ALG1_U02_L01_GP04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L01_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Solve q/5 = -7. {
~ q = -12#This subtracts 5, but division by 5 is undone by multiplication by 5.
~ q = -2#This adds 5 instead of multiplying by 5.
~ q = 35#This uses the right operation but loses the negative sign.
= q = -35#Correct. Multiply both sides by 5: q = -35.
}

::ALG1_U02_L01_GP05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L01_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>A number decreased by 11 is 2. Which value is the number? {
= 13#Correct. n - 11 = 2, so add 11 to get n = 13.
~ 9#Check: 9 - 11 = -2, not 2.
~ -13#Check: -13 - 11 = -24, not 2.
~ 22#This doubles 11 instead of solving n - 11 = 2.
}
